      <h1>Manufacturer Verification Criteria</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Manufacturer Directory · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A manufacturer appears in this directory only after its <strong>company
        identity</strong>, <strong>certifications</strong> and <strong>product data</strong> are checked against public
        sources. A listing states what was verified and what was not — it does not claim to be a full due-diligence audit.
      </div>

      <h2>What "verified" means here</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>What is looked for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Company identity</td><td>Registered name, country, and a traceable public footprint (company register, website, export records)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Certifications</td><td>Named standards only — e.g. ISO 9001, IATF 16949, IEC, CE — with the issuing context, never a bare "certified" claim</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Product data</td><td>Model specifications that state their test standard (a CCA value without JIS/EN/SAE/DIN is not recorded)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The claimed-vs-accredited distinction</h2>
      <p>
        The single most important distinction in this directory is between what a manufacturer <strong>claims</strong>
        and what is <strong>independently accredited</strong>. A manufacturer may advertise "ISO 9001" or "CE" without
        the certification being verifiable, and a testing capability may be advertised without the corresponding
        accreditation (e.g. ISO/IEC 17025). This directory records a certification as <em>verified</em> only when it
        can be traced to a named standard and issuing context; otherwise it is marked <strong>unverified</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>The verification checklist</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Name and registration</strong> — is the company a real, traceable legal entity?</li>
        <li><strong>Certification</strong> — is each claimed standard named and traceable to an issuer?</li>
        <li><strong>Product spec</strong> — does each figure state its test standard (CCA basis, capacity rate)?</li>
        <li><strong>Factory / capacity claims</strong> — is there a source, or is it marketing language?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What this directory is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It is <strong>not</strong> a certification authority or an audit report.</li>
        <li>It does <strong>not</strong> rank manufacturers or endorse purchases.</li>
        <li>It does <strong>not</strong> repeat an unverified marketing claim as fact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A manufacturer that claims a certification this directory cannot substantiate is listed as <strong>unverified</strong>
      on that point, not silently credited with it.</p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> A battery manufacturer directory earns trust exactly by admitting what it could
        not verify — and the whole game is the claimed-vs-accredited line, because "certified" is only as good as the
        check behind it.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The battery trade is full of bare "ISO/CE" badges and unaccredited test claims. The
        useful, honest signal is a named standard plus a source, and a directory that distinguishes verified from
        unverified is the only kind worth reading — for a buyer, that one distinction is worth more than a hundred
        marketing pages.</p>
